---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { PERSON_ID } from '../../lib/schema';

const advisory = {
	'@type': 'Service',
	'@id': 'https://okarthur.com/work/#advisory',
	name: 'Continuing advisory',
	serviceType:
		'Independent advisory on digital, data and AI in infrastructure',
	description:
		'Retained independent advice to infrastructure owners and leadership teams on digital, data and AI decisions and procurements, bought as a retainer or a defined block of days.',
	url: 'https://okarthur.com/work/',
	provider: { '@id': PERSON_ID },
	areaServed: [
		{ '@type': 'Country', name: 'United Arab Emirates' },
		{ '@type': 'Country', name: 'United Kingdom' },
		{ '@type': 'Place', name: 'Gulf Cooperation Council' },
	],
	audience: {
		'@type': 'Audience',
		audienceType:
			'Infrastructure owners, developers and major capital programmes',
	},
};
---

<Base
	title="Advisory, review and board work"
	description="Independent judgement from Steven Yule on an infrastructure decision before it is confirmed. A written review, continuing advisory or a board appointment."
	schema={[advisory]}
>
	<div class="page-head">
		<h1 class="page-title">Work</h1>
		<p class="lede">
			Three ways to commission independent judgement on a decision.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="prose numbered">
		<p>
			The three differ mainly in how long the advice is needed. A decision
			taken at one point suits a review, which ends in a written opinion.
			Decisions that keep arriving over months suit a retained adviser, and
			decisions that sit with a board suit a non-executive appointment.
		</p>

		<h2 id="review">Independent review</h2>
		<p>
			A review gives a second opinion on one decision before it is confirmed.
			The decision might be a supplier's proposal, a business case or a
			handover into operation, or a change of plan at group level that
			touches more than one development. Scope and end date are fixed before
			work starts, and so is the fee, which I propose after a first call. The
			output is one written opinion, short enough for a board to read and
			specific enough to act on.
		</p>
		<p>
			<a href="/review/">The review page</a> sets out the full scope of a
			review, including what sits outside it.
		</p>

		<h2 id="advisory">Continuing advisory</h2>
		<p>
			Some decisions do not resolve in a single opinion. Where an owner or a
			leadership team needs independent advice over several months, I work as
			a retained adviser. That can mean client-side support while a supplier
			delivers, or setting the scope and evaluation criteria of a platform
			procurement before it goes to market. It can also mean challenging a
			digital or data strategy before it is funded. On some programmes it
			means being the person who will say what nobody in the room is paid to
			say.
		</p>
		<p>
			Advisory is bought as a retainer or as a defined block of days, agreed
			in advance. The independence rule that governs a review applies here
			too. I take no delivery work and no supplier-side work on anything I am
			advising on, and any interest is declared before the engagement starts.
		</p>

		<h2 id="board">Board and non-executive</h2>
		<p>
			Boards are being asked to approve digital and AI investment at a scale
			most of them cannot test. They are also asked to hold a portfolio of
			developments to a plan using reports written one development at a time.
			A board asked to approve a data platform usually sees a business case
			written by the people who will deliver it, and few members are in a
			position to question its assumptions.
		</p>
		<p>
			I take non-executive and advisory board appointments where that is the
			gap, with private companies and advisory boards in the UK and the UAE. I
			am a non-executive director of Thomas Telford Ltd, and I bring to a
			board a chartered engineer's career on the digital side of capital
			delivery.
		</p>

		<h2 id="speaking">Speaking and writing</h2>
		<p>
			I speak and write on digital, data and AI in infrastructure, and I am
			available for press comment. Recent talks and published work are listed
			under <a href="/elsewhere/">Published elsewhere</a>, and the <a
				href="/media/">media page</a
			> has a biography and a photo for organisers and editors.
		</p>

		<h2 id="start-here">Start here</h2>
		<p>
			<a href="/contact/">Tell me the decision you are facing</a>, or the role
			you are trying to fill.
		</p>
	</div>
</Base>
